EXT. DRAINED MUNICIPAL SWIMMING POOL - DAY

A company retreat held in the deep end of a decommissioned pool. Folding chairs are arranged in a circle on the sloped floor, tilted slightly toward the drain. Lane lines coil in a corner like dead snakes. A banner on the diving board reads: SYNERGY THROUGH SPLASH.

FACILITATOR PRIYA DESHMUKH-WALL, 40s, tailored linen, a headset mic taped to her cheek, stands in the exact center on the drain cover. Around her sit four employees: BARNES, 50s, sales director, in a polo with a company crest; LEOCADIA, 30s, product lead, cross-legged on her chair; WITOLD, 20s, an intern sipping an iced coffee; and DESMOND "DEZ" ROUSE, 40s, finance, who tugs at his collar and checks his watch every ninety seconds.

PRIYA
Everyone, I want you to close your eyes and picture water. Warm, clear, still water.

BARNES
(whispering to Leocadia)
It's a pool.

LEOCADIA
(whispering back)
It's a drained pool.

WITOLD
(brightly)
Can I picture the empty version?

PRIYA
You may picture whatever serves you.

Priya paces a slow arc around the circle, her sandals slapping on the tile. Barnes opens one eye and peeks at Dez. Dez peeks back. Leocadia sips her water bottle and rearranges her crossed legs. Witold's chair tilts and he catches the diving-board ladder with one hand without opening his eyes.

DEZ
(muttering)
We've done this twice already.

PRIYA
Dez, I'm hearing resistance.

DEZ
I'm hearing a leaf blower.

Everyone listens. A distant whine over the pool wall. Priya smiles, patient.

PRIYA
We call that the sound of the world continuing.

BARNES
Can we get to the part with the numbers?

PRIYA
There are no numbers today.

BARNES
Then why am I here?

PRIYA
(to the group)
Because you want to be seen.

Barnes folds his arms. A long silence. Witold's iced coffee crackles as the ice shifts. Leocadia clears her throat and raises her hand like a schoolchild.

LEOCADIA
Priya, I think I'm going to say something and I need everyone to just let me.

PRIYA
This is a safe deep end.

LEOCADIA
We lost the Hartwell contract. Last Tuesday. The CEO told us not to say anything.

The circle stiffens. Barnes' arms drop. Dez stops fiddling with his collar. Witold lowers his coffee.

DEZ
(very quietly)
Oh God.

BARNES
How do you know?

LEOCADIA
I wrote the termination letter.

A beat. Priya, for the first time all morning, says nothing at all. The leaf blower whines on, rising and falling. Then Barnes begins, slowly, to laugh. He wheezes, shoulders shaking, tears in his eyes, and the others catch it one by one, even Dez, even Witold, a ragged communal howl that echoes off the tile.

PRIYA
(softly, into her mic)
I think we've just had a breakthrough.

BARNES
(still laughing)
Priya, we've had a firing.
